# GitHub Actions cost discipline

Treat GitHub-hosted jobs as metered compute.

- Dormant/stored code: no scheduled Actions.
- Prefer event, webhook, relevant code-change, release or manual workflow_dispatch.
- Batch logically related changes before pushing.
- Validate locally/in-agent before remote CI where practical.
- Never create commits merely to trigger CI.
- Never manually rerun an equivalent CI run that is already queued/running.
- Add PR concurrency/cancel-in-progress where safe so superseded code does not keep consuming minutes.
- Use changed-path filters where safe.
- Fast validation during active PR work; full validation at main/release/dependency/security gates.
- Never weaken required release/security coverage to save money.
- No cron/polling "just in case".
- Live-service uptime checks belong on the cheapest appropriate central monitor, not GitHub Actions, unless GitHub is genuinely required.
- Autonomous agents wake for real work/events, not merely because a timer expired.
- Do not raise spend limits to mask inefficient workflow design.

Before every push ask: "Does this push contain enough completed, validated work to justify another CI run?"
